From d8529031090d993015e64b24edb0ee8e79c5fb7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kycube <kf1178@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:04:25 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 21/36] target/xtensa: TCG memory order TSO, so two cores see
 stores in program order

TCG_GUEST_DEFAULT_MO was 0 ("Xtensa processors have a weak memory model"),
so under MTTCG on a weakly ordered host no barrier separates two guest
stores and the other vCPU may see them in either order. The ESP32-S3's
LX7 cores are in-order and ESP-IDF relies on its stores becoming visible
in program order: spinlock_release() frees a lock with a plain store, and
esp_cpu_compare_and_set() for an address in external RAM takes a software
lock with S32C1I, compares and stores the lock word, then releases the
software lock with a plain store right after it.

On an arm64 host the stock xteink firmware (ESP-IDF 6.0.1) aborted in about
half of its boots with six emulators running:

  assert failed: 0x40384078 <cached disabled>:141 (<cached disabled>)

spinlock.h:141 is assert(lock->owner == core_owner_id) after a successful
compare-and-set. Stopped there (gdbstub breakpoint): CPU0 had taken a
queue's spinlock at 0x3C6F97E8 (PSRAM) and read the owner back as 0xABAB,
CPU1's id. CPU1's release of the software lock had become visible before
its store of the lock word, so CPU0 compared against the stale FREE value.

A message-passing litmus on the two emulated cores (data store, flag
store, no MEMW between them; the other core waits for the flag, MEMW,
reads the data) saw the new flag with the old data 78..342 times per
million round trips with MO 0 or TCG_MO_LD_LD, and never with
TCG_MO_ST_ST or TSO. Stock boots, 6 instances x 8: 26/48 asserted with
MO 0, 22/48 with LD_LD, 0/48 with ST_ST, 0/48 with TSO.

TSO (every order but store->load) is what an in-order core with a FIFO
store buffer provides. It emits nothing on x86 hosts, which is why the
assert never showed on Linux CI; on arm64 it costs a DMB per guest load
and store (a memory-bound loop ran about 13% slower, a stock boot used
about 8% more host CPU).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 target/xtensa/cpu-param.h | 17 +++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/target/xtensa/cpu-param.h b/target/xtensa/cpu-param.h
index 0000725..8ecb738 100644
--- a/target/xtensa/cpu-param.h
+++ b/target/xtensa/cpu-param.h
@@ -17,7 +17,20 @@
 #define TARGET_VIRT_ADDR_SPACE_BITS 32
 #endif
 
-/* Xtensa processors have a weak memory model */
-#define TCG_GUEST_DEFAULT_MO      (0)
+/*
+ * The ISA leaves the order of ordinary loads and stores to different
+ * addresses open, but the SMP parts QEMU runs real firmware for (the
+ * ESP32-S3's two in-order LX7 cores) make them visible in program order,
+ * except that a load may pass an earlier store, and ESP-IDF depends on it:
+ * spinlock_release() frees a lock with a plain store, and the
+ * compare-and-set it uses for locks in external RAM releases its software
+ * lock with a plain store right after the store it protects. With MTTCG on
+ * a weakly ordered host (arm64) the second store could become visible
+ * first, the other core then read a stale lock word, and both cores owned
+ * the same PSRAM spinlock (ESP-IDF's spinlock.h:141 assert,
+ * "lock->owner == core_owner_id"). TSO here costs nothing on x86 hosts and
+ * a DMB per guest load and store on arm64.
+ */
+#define TCG_GUEST_DEFAULT_MO      (TCG_MO_ALL & ~TCG_MO_ST_LD)
 
 #endif
